.TH KESTREL_HINT 2 "2026-10-22" "Kestrel" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
sched_ext_hint \- per-task scheduler hint (Kestrel)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <linux/sched/types.h>
.B #include <linux/sched.h>

.B #define SCHED_FLAG_KESTREL_HINT (1ULL << 40)
.B #define SCHED_KESTREL_HINT_MAX  1024
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sched_setattr (2)
and
.BR sched_getattr (2)
carry an extra
.I __u32
field,
.IR sched_ext_hint ,
after
.IR sched_util_max .
A task sets it by calling
.BR sched_setattr (2)
with
.B SCHED_FLAG_KESTREL_HINT
in
.I sched_flags
and the value in
.IR sched_ext_hint ;
the value is in the range
.RB [ 0 ,
.BR SCHED_KESTREL_HINT_MAX ].
Values outside that range are rejected with
.BR EINVAL .

The hint is opaque to the scheduler core. A
.BR sched_ext (7)
scheduler reads it with the
.B scx_bpf_task_hint ()
kfunc and maps it to a scheduling policy; a value of zero means "no hint".
The field is versioned by
.IR sched_attr.size ,
so callers that pass an older
.I size
are unaffected and read zero.

Callers are expected to set
.B SCHED_FLAG_KEEP_POLICY
and
.B SCHED_FLAG_KEEP_PARAMS
alongside
.B SCHED_FLAG_KESTREL_HINT
so that setting a hint does not disturb the task's scheduling policy,
priority or nice value.

On a kernel without this extension,
.BR sched_setattr (2)
returns
.B EINVAL
for the unknown flag, and
.BR sched_getattr (2)
returns a
.I size
that does not cover the field.
.SH RETURN VALUE
These interfaces return zero on success; on error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set. The error codes are those of
.BR sched_setattr (2)
and
.BR sched_getattr (2);
.B EINVAL
also indicates that the running kernel has no Kestrel hint support.
.SH CONFORMING TO
Kestrel-specific, not in POSIX.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sched_setattr (2),
.BR sched (7)
